# Troubleshooting Workflow

The main troubleshooting process for Lab 07 was:

```text
Storage access problem
        ↓
Check Storage Account configuration
        ↓
Check Public Network Access
        ↓
Check Private Endpoint
        ↓
Check Private Endpoint IP
        ↓
Check Private DNS
        ↓
Check DNS resolution
        ↓
Check TCP 443 connectivity
        ↓
Check authentication
        ↓
Check RBAC
        ↓
Check Storage API response
```

This helped separate different types of problems:

```text
DNS problem
    ↓
Hostname resolves incorrectly

Network problem
    ↓
Cannot connect to private IP / port 443

Authentication problem
    ↓
Token or identity problem

Authorization problem
    ↓
RBAC does not allow the operation

Application/API problem
    ↓
Storage API returns an HTTP error
```

---

# Management Plane vs Data Plane

One of the important concepts from this lab was the difference between the Azure management plane and data plane.

```text
Management Plane
       ↓
Azure Resource Manager
       ↓
Manage resources
       ↓
Storage Account configuration
```

versus:

```text
Data Plane
       ↓
Storage service
       ↓
Read / write Blob or File data
```

For example:

```text
az storage account show
```

can manage or inspect the Storage Account through Azure Resource Manager.

But:

```text
Blob data access
```

requires access to the Storage data plane.

This distinction helped explain why Cloud Shell could manage the Storage Account while being unable to directly access Blob data when public network access was disabled.

---

# Managed Identity vs RBAC

Another important concept was the difference between authentication and authorization.

```text
Managed Identity
       ↓
Who are you?
```

```text
RBAC
       ↓
What are you allowed to do?
```

The complete flow was:

```text
VM
 ↓
Managed Identity
 ↓
Access Token
 ↓
Azure Storage
 ↓
Check RBAC
 ↓
Storage Blob Data Contributor
 ↓
Allow Blob operation
```

The VM's Managed Identity was not manually combined with the token.

Azure issued the token based on the VM's identity, and Azure Storage used the identity represented by the token when evaluating RBAC permissions.

